## Top 5 Pitfalls Web & Backend Engineers Must Avoid

<div class="portal-alert-box">
  <div class="portal-alert-title">SECURITY &amp; STABILITY PITFALLS</div>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1. Never Hardcode API Keys or Secrets:</strong> Client application binaries are public assets. Anyone can decompile an <code>.apk</code> or <code>.ipa</code> package and extract string constants. Store tokens exclusively in KeyStore/Keychain or resolve them at runtime via OAuth SSO.</li>
    <li><strong>2. Never Run Heavy Loops on the Main Thread:</strong> Long computations, synchronous file I/O, or parsing large JSON payloads freeze the screen, triggering OS crashes. Always dispatch work to background workers or coroutine dispatchers.</li>
    <li><strong>3. Never Assume Continuous Connectivity:</strong> Always handle offline states, network timeouts, and flaky cellular signals. Provide local caching and retry mechanisms.</li>
    <li><strong>4. Clean Up Event Listeners to Prevent Memory Leaks:</strong> Mobile OS devices have constrained RAM. Dangling event subscribers, uncancelled timers, and retained singleton delegates keep screens in memory, causing OOM (Out-of-Memory) termination.</li>
    <li><strong>5. Never Build Production Binaries on Personal Laptops:</strong> Official application packages must be built, signed, and distributed exclusively through automated Bitrise CI pipelines to guarantee tamper-proof audit trails.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

